<spell-notebook "video usage">
	<header level="1">
		Video
		<header>A video plays a video file in the browser's own player.</>
	</>
	<video-tab-bar showing="usage"/>
	<section>
		<menu class="ui-docs-section-tabs" appearance="segmented" aria-label="Sections">
			<item link="#using-it">Using it</>
			<item link="#accessibility">Accessibility</>
		</>
	</>

	<!-- USAGE:  how to use it (attributes in practice, events, framework snippets), keyboard, accessibility. -->
	<section label="Using it">
		<markdown>
			`<ui-video>` plays a video file in the browser's own player.  For another site's video (YouTube, Vimeo), use
			[`<ui-embed>`](/components/ui/ui-embed.html).

			- `src` is the file, relative to the page;  `poster` a picture shown before it plays.
			- The browser's controls show unless `controls="false"`.
			- `captions` is a captions file (WebVTT, `.vtt`), in the page's language:  the player offers them.
			- `ratio` is its shape, width to height (`16/9`);  by default the video's own.
			- `autoplay` starts it at once, but a browser only allows that when it's `muted`, or after the reader has clicked
			  on the page.  There's no way round that, on purpose.
			- `muted` and `loop` are the player's own.

			From script:  `video.play()`, `video.pause()`, `video.currentTime`, `video.paused`, and the events `ui-play`,
			`ui-pause` and `ui-ended`.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Accessibility">
		<markdown>
			- Name it with `label`:  what the video shows, for people who can't see it.
			- Give it `captions` when it has speech:  people who can't hear it read them.
			- The browser's controls are keyboard-ready:  Tab to them, Space to play and pause.
		</>
	</>
</spell-notebook>
